{
  "evidence": "TEXT_F: >£30bn extra NHS funding, ring‑fenced mental health budgets, £8bn more for social care, and a plan to end rough sleeping; end freedom of movement, 10,000 more police, 500 more border guards, defence spend ≥2% of GDP and renew the nuclear deterrent; scrap tuition fees, restore maintenance grants, Living Wage ≥£10, ban zero‑hours contracts, sectoral bargaining, and stronger equality enforcement; crack down on tax avoidance, higher taxes for top earners and large corporations, restore small profits corporation tax rate, and curb excessive pay; Clean Air Act, ban fracking, protect habitats. TEXT_G: £2bn increase for Scotland’s NHS, oppose welfare cuts, uprate benefits, scrap the two‑child cap, increase Carer’s Allowance; cancel nuclear weapons renewal, seek additional devolved powers, and affirm the Scottish Parliament’s right to decide on an independence referendum; protect EU health recruitment, continued EMA participation, refugee protection and integration, free university tuition in Scotland; raise the Additional Rate to 50p, tax bankers’ bonuses, oppose further Corporation Tax cuts, and strengthen tax collection; moratoria on fracking and prioritise renewables.",
  "reasoning": "Because the profile prioritises Care, Loyalty, and Equality, TEXT_F aligns better by coupling expansive NHS/social‑care commitments and worker protections (Care) with stronger national security, policing, border control, and a retained nuclear deterrent (Loyalty/Authority), alongside broad equality measures like free higher education and pay‑gap enforcement (Equality), and proportional fairness via anti‑avoidance and progressive taxation. TEXT_G strongly reflects Care and Equality too, but its cancellation of nuclear renewal and emphasis on Scottish independence/devolution signal weaker alignment with UK‑wide Loyalty/Authority priorities in this profile.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_F"
}
